mov: an interrupt in a fragment's header atoms is no end of the input

mov_switch_root() cleared next_root_atom before it read the next
fragment's moof. When the caller's interrupt callback cut that read (the
open of an hls segment, or its first bytes, while a player pauses with
its prefetch interrupt), mov_read_default() stopped on the latched error
and AVERROR_EOF went up as AVERROR_EXIT; but with next_root_atom 0 the
next call took mov_read_packet()'s "no next root atom" for the end of
the input, and hls ended the playlist on the first read after the
interrupt: a long pause on CMAF HLS that began while the demuxer stood
between two fragments ended playback on resume. An interrupt inside the
moof was worse to retry: the parse had taken the missing bytes for zeros
and put the fragment's samples in the index, which a second parse would
add again.

Before parsing, read the atoms up to and with the next mdat's header into
the I/O buffer and seek back (mov_preload_root(); ffio_ensure_seekback()
makes the buffer keep them, its window doubled as needed; at most 32
atoms and 16 MiB, beyond which the parse reads as before). When the
interrupt cuts that read, nothing is parsed: the fragment's headers_read
is undone, AVERROR_EXIT is returned, and the next call reads the root
again from the same position (hls's reset path at position 0 too). A
seek drops the pending read. The parse itself then reads only from the
buffer. Not on a seekable input (a progressive file, a DASH or CMAF .mp4
whose sidx makes mov switch roots at every fragment): there the seeks
would go to the protocol where the atoms span the buffer (for http a new
connection with a Range request), and an interrupt's error stays in the
input's context for good (hls alone clears it), so there is nothing to
read again; such an input reads as before.

